From: Mirdispo contributors
Subject: Raise the video bitrate limit to 10 Mbit/s

The video bitrate was capped at 4 Mbit/s, meant to keep the Wi-Fi link from
saturating. For a 1080p picture that is little: whenever much of the screen
moves, as when playing video or dragging a window, x264 has to quantise so
hard that the picture smears and turns blocky.

The cap is now 10 Mbit/s. That is below the lowest maximum a receiver can
announce (14 Mbit/s for level 3.1) and small next to what a Wi-Fi link
carries, and the receiver's own announced maximum still applies when it is
lower.

diff -ruN a/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c b/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
--- a/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
+++ b/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
@@ -612,10 +612,12 @@
   guint gop_size = resolution->refresh_rate;
   guint bitrate_kbit = wfd_video_codec_get_max_bitrate_kbit (codec);
 
-  /* Limit initial video bitrate to 512kBit/s to ensure we don't
-   * saturate the wifi link.
-   * This is a rather bad method, but it kind of works. */
-  bitrate_kbit = MIN (bitrate_kbit, 512 * 8);
+  /* Limit the video bitrate to stay well clear of what the Wi-Fi link can
+   * carry. This used to be 4 Mbit/s, which leaves a 1080p picture smeared
+   * and blocky whenever much of it moves. 10 Mbit/s is below the lowest
+   * maximum a receiver can announce (14 Mbit/s) and small next to what a
+   * Wi-Fi link carries. */
+  bitrate_kbit = MIN (bitrate_kbit, 10 * 1024);
 
   if (resolution->interlaced)
     g_warning ("Resolution should never be set to interlaced as that is not supported with all codecs.");
